              <section class="operations-critique" aria-label="Potential benefits">
                <article class="operations-panel operations-footprint" data-reveal-step="1">
                  <h3><svg class="refinement-icon heading-icon" viewBox="0 0 48 48" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="12" y="12" width="24" height="24" rx="4"/><path d="M18 18h12v12H18zM18 4v8m12-8v8M18 36v8m12-8v8M4 18h8m-8 12h8m24-12h8m-8 12h8"/></svg>Digital footprint</h3>
                  <p class="operations-benefit"><span>Potential benefit</span>More efficient resource allocation.</p>
                  <p class="operations-citation">Bibri &amp; Krogstie (2017) · Lange &amp; Santarius (2020)</p>
                </article>
                <article class="operations-panel operations-people" data-reveal-step="2">
                  <h3><svg class="refinement-icon heading-icon" viewBox="0 0 48 48" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="17" cy="15" r="6"/><circle cx="33" cy="17" r="5"/><path d="M5 39v-4c0-7 5-11 12-11s12 4 12 11v4H5Zm26-13c7 0 12 4 12 10v3H33"/></svg>People and work</h3>
                  <p class="operations-benefit"><span>Potential benefit</span>More responsive services and reduced unnecessary cleaning tasks.</p>
                  <p class="operations-citation">Group field visit to SIT (2026) · Trencher (2019)</p>
                </article>
              </section>

              <p class="operations-takeaway" data-reveal-step="3">Operational productivity does not automatically establish human-centred or net environmental benefits.</p>
              <footer class="operations-sources" data-reveal-step="3">Sources: Bibri &amp; Krogstie (2017) · Group field visit to SIT (2026) · Lange &amp; Santarius (2020) · Trencher (2019)</footer>
